-- Umbra — on-ledger FOUR-EYES clearing approval (IDEN-03).
--
-- `ClearingApproval` is the on-ledger four-eyes credential that gates SETTLEMENT:
-- an operator/Compliance-signed attestation carrying the round id + the approved
-- clearing price. `Round.Clear` REQUIRES a matching one before it commits the
-- Batch DvP — a settle with no / wrong-price / wrong-round / operator-self-made
-- approval aborts ON-LEDGER. It is the four-eyes GATE PRIMITIVE; the enforcement
-- (fetch the credential by ContractId + `assertClearingApproved` inside
-- `Round.Clear`, immediately after the §8 recompute pins `priceDec`, before the
-- Batch is built) lives in Umbra.Auction. This module ships the request template
-- + the two-party approval template + the `assertClearingApproved` gate helper.
--
-- This is byte-for-byte the `DeskEligibility` mechanism already shipped in
-- `Compliance.daml` (`signatory operator, compliance` + fetched-by-ContractId +
-- an `assert…` gate). Operator is a signatory of the approval, so the `fetch`
-- inside `Round.Clear` (controller operator) is authorized exactly as the
-- `DeskEligibility` fetch inside `Venue.SubmitOrder` is.
--
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- FOUR-EYES SEPARATION (the tested control): `ClearingApproval` is signed by BOTH
-- `operator` AND `compliance`, and it can be brought into being ONLY by the
-- `compliance` party exercising `ApproveClearing` on a request the operator
-- proposed. The operator ALONE cannot forge a `ClearingApproval` — the distinct
-- `compliance` signatory is the whole point (a self-approving operator would be a
-- four-eyes bypass). `operator` and `compliance` MUST be DISTINCT parties for the
-- separation to be real; the negative test `test_operator_cannot_self_approve`
-- proves the operator cannot create one alone.
--
-- DEV NOTE (honest): for the fast local loop / headless `daml test`, the
-- `compliance` party may be a DEDICATED dev party (allocated alongside the
-- operator) so the tests and headless runs proceed without a human in the loop.
-- The signatory SEPARATION stays REAL regardless — a dedicated dev compliance
-- party is still a distinct authority the operator does not control. Live human
-- four-eyes (a real Compliance operator approving in the UI) is Wave-5 + live UAT.
--
-- NO CONTRACT KEYS (LF 2.1 — D7 / 11-02 environmental deviation): the approval is
-- threaded into `Round.Clear` by explicit ContractId (the D7 Option-B discipline
-- already used for every Asset/Holding leg), NOT via `fetchByKey`.
--
-- Standalone module (no Auction/Holding import) so it compiles independently.
module Umbra.Approval where

-- Operator PROPOSES a clearing to Compliance (carries the deterministically-
-- recomputed §8 price). Signed by operator only; Compliance is an observer so it
-- can see the pending clearing and act on it. This is the "request" half of the
-- propose/approve two-step that supplies the compliance authority.
template ClearingApprovalRequest
  with
    operator      : Party    -- venue operator (proposes the clearing)
    compliance    : Party    -- Compliance role (distinct authority — approves/rejects)
    roundId       : Text      -- the round this approval is scoped to
    clearingPrice : Decimal   -- the deterministically-recomputed §8 price
  where
    signatory operator
    observer compliance

    -- Compliance APPROVES → the two-party-signed `ClearingApproval` is born.
    -- Compliance authority co-flows here (controller compliance), so the
    -- operator+compliance signatory set on `ClearingApproval` is satisfied.
    -- Operator ALONE cannot create it — the four-eyes SEPARATION.
    choice ApproveClearing : ContractId ClearingApproval
      controller compliance
      do
        -- FOUR-EYES DISTINCTNESS (on-ledger, fail-loud): the compliance approver
        -- MUST be a DISTINCT party from the operator. If a deployment collapses the
        -- two ids (compliance == operator), this aborts HERE — the operator can no
        -- longer mint a self-approving `ClearingApproval`, even when it holds both
        -- authorities. The separation is now a LEDGER invariant, not merely a
        -- property of the test's party choice.
        assertMsg "four-eyes requires a distinct compliance party (operator /= compliance)"
          (compliance /= operator)
        create ClearingApproval with operator; compliance; roundId; clearingPrice

    -- Compliance may REJECT (consuming; no approval is created → the settle path
    -- aborts for lack of a matching `ClearingApproval`).
    choice RejectClearing : ()
      controller compliance
      do pure ()

-- The four-eyes credential `Round.Clear` REQUIRES. Signed by BOTH operator and
-- compliance (unforgeable by operator alone). Carries the round + the approved
-- price so the gate can pin both.
template ClearingApproval
  with
    operator      : Party    -- venue operator (settles under sole operator authority)
    compliance    : Party    -- Compliance co-signer (the distinct four-eyes authority)
    roundId       : Text      -- the round this approval authorizes settlement of
    clearingPrice : Decimal   -- the Compliance-approved clearing price
  where
    signatory operator, compliance

-- The GATE PRIMITIVE `Round.Clear` calls (keyless — fetched by ContractId, D7
-- Option-B). Mirrors `assertDeskEligible`: asserts the approval is for THIS
-- operator/round AND matches the already-rounded `priceDec` that `Round.Clear` is
-- about to settle. A missing / wrong-price / wrong-round approval aborts ON-LEDGER
-- (assertMsg failure), never in render logic. The caller passes the already
-- half-even-rounded `priceDec` (the same `roundBankers 2 clearingPrice` the §8
-- recompute pinned), so both sides are compared at 2dp.
assertClearingApproved : Party -> Text -> Decimal -> ClearingApproval -> Update ()
assertClearingApproved operator roundId priceDec appr = do
  -- FOUR-EYES DISTINCTNESS (on-ledger, fail-loud): even the SETTLE gate re-checks
  -- that the approval's compliance signatory is DISTINCT from the operator. A
  -- collapsed-party `ClearingApproval` (compliance == operator) — however it came
  -- into being — is rejected at `Round.Clear`, so operator self-approval can never
  -- authorize a settlement. Belt-and-suspenders with the `ApproveClearing` guard.
  assertMsg "four-eyes requires a distinct compliance party (operator /= compliance)"
    (appr.compliance /= operator)
  assertMsg "approval is not for this operator/round"
    (appr.operator == operator && appr.roundId == roundId)
  assertMsg "compliance-approved price does not match the clearing price"
    (roundBankers 2 appr.clearingPrice == priceDec)
